# .claude/preferences.yaml — personal code review taste/style lens
#
# Copy this file to your home directory at: ~/.claude/preferences.yaml
# All entries are optional and commented out. Uncomment and edit what applies to you.
#
# ─── What is this? ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Preferences are a LENS through which reviewers shape how findings are worded and
# prioritized — they are never a suppression list. A preference influences *how* a
# finding is framed and its emphasis in the report, but never eliminates a finding
# or dilutes its severity.
#
# Hard floor: preferences never dilute CRITICAL or security-flagged finding severity.
# This is the same floor that decisions.yaml held. When a finding is security-domain
# or CRITICAL, it passes through unaffected.
#
# Project-level wins on conflict: when .claude/project.yaml or a project-level ruling
# conflicts with a preference here, the project-level rule wins. Preferences are
# personal defaults; projects can override them.
#
# Reviewers should note in their reasoning when a preference shaped their wording
# (e.g., "per user preference `no-magic-strings`") so the review stays reproducible.
#
# ─── Schema ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Each entry is a rule with the following fields:
#
# - name: A short identifier (kebab-case), matching [a-z0-9-]+
# - rule: The rule itself, 1-2 sentences. What it is or what it asks for.
# - spirit: The reasoning behind the rule. Why this matters to you.
# - appliesTo: Which reviewers should apply it, or "all" for everyone.
#   Examples: "all", "tara-typesafe", "uncle-bob,security-sage"
# - revisitIf: A condition under which this rule should be questioned.
#   Examples: "the project's type system is very dynamic",
#             "strict perf is a hard requirement"

# ─── Examples (uncomment and customize) ────────────────────────────────────

# # Preference: no magic strings
# - name: no-magic-strings
#   rule: >
#     Avoid magic strings in code. Extract them to named constants at the top of the file
#     or in a constants module.
#   spirit: >
#     Magic strings are maintenance landmines. When the same string appears in tests, docs,
#     and code, they drift. Naming them makes them visible, testable, and changeable.
#   appliesTo: all
#   revisitIf: the project has a string-heavy dynamic approach and constants would be overkill

# # Preference: no `as` in TypeScript
# - name: no-as-in-ts
#   rule: >
#     Avoid `as` type assertions in TypeScript. Use `satisfies` or narrow types with guards instead.
#   spirit: >
#     Type assertions bypass the type system. They hide bugs. A `satisfies` check or a proper
#     type guard keeps the compiler in the loop.
#   appliesTo: tara-typesafe
#   revisitIf: you're at a third-party boundary where `as` is the only sane option

# # Preference: readability over cleverness
# - name: readability-over-cleverness
#   rule: >
#     Prefer explicit, readable code over clever one-liners. A loop that takes 5 lines is
#     better than a nested comprehension that takes 1.
#   spirit: >
#     Code is read more than it is written. A clever line saves 4 lines of code but costs
#     the next reader 10 minutes. Readability compounds.
#   appliesTo: uncle-bob
#   revisitIf: you're in a competitive programming context or performance is the only concern
